1
00:00:02,710 --> 00:00:06,290
So I told Carl, nobody, no matter how famous their parents are...

2
00:00:06,460 --> 00:00:08,920
...nobody is allowed to climb on the dinosaur.

3
00:00:09,090 --> 00:00:11,420
But of course, this went right in one ear...

4
00:00:11,590 --> 00:00:14,340
I love how he cares so much about stuff.

5
00:00:14,510 --> 00:00:18,470
If I squint, I can pretend he's Alan Alda.

6
00:00:20,810 --> 00:00:23,890
Oh, good. Another dinosaur story.

7
00:00:25,810 --> 00:00:28,020
When are those gonna become extinct?

8
00:00:30,980 --> 00:00:34,650
If I was a superhero who could fly and be invisible...

9
00:00:34,820 --> 00:00:37,070
...that would be the best.

10
00:00:41,790 --> 00:00:44,290
What does Rachel see in this guy?

11
00:00:46,210 --> 00:00:47,620
I love Rachel.

12
00:00:50,460 --> 00:00:52,590
I wish she was my wife.

13
00:01:05,140 --> 00:01:06,810
Who's singing?

14
00:01:58,860 --> 00:02:01,990
Hey, when you guys were kids and you played Happy Days...

15
00:02:02,200 --> 00:02:05,870
...who were you? I was always Richie.

16
00:02:02,200 --> 00:02:05,870
I was always Joanie.

17
00:02:06,040 --> 00:02:10,290
Was, uh, "Egg the Gellers" the war cry of your neighborhood?

18
00:02:12,250 --> 00:02:14,040
Ew, ew! It's the Mattress King.

19
00:02:14,210 --> 00:02:15,880
Don't look. Change the channel.

20
00:02:16,050 --> 00:02:18,670
Wait. I want to see this. After I divorce him...

21
00:02:18,840 --> 00:02:21,970
...half of that kingdom is gonna be mine.

22
00:02:22,300 --> 00:02:25,220
Despair fills the mattress showroom.

23
00:02:25,390 --> 00:02:28,310
My kingdom is suddenly without a queen.

24
00:02:28,480 --> 00:02:30,060
I'm so depressed...

25
00:02:30,230 --> 00:02:31,810
...I'm going to slash...

26
00:02:31,980 --> 00:02:34,110
...my prices! Check it out!

27
00:02:34,270 --> 00:02:38,360
599 for a California king. 499 for a pillow-top queen set.

28
00:02:38,530 --> 00:02:40,820
I'm going medieval on prices.

29
00:02:40,990 --> 00:02:42,870
What a wank.

30
00:02:43,030 --> 00:02:47,080
Oh, I cannot believe he's using our divorce to sell mattresses.

31
00:02:47,250 --> 00:02:51,420
I know. At 499 for a pillow-top queen, who cares about the divorce?

32
00:02:51,580 --> 00:02:53,920
Those babies will sell themselves.

33
00:02:56,050 --> 00:02:58,880
And I'm appalled for you, by the way.

34
00:03:00,090 --> 00:03:03,430
I'm close. I'm cheap. I'm the king.

35
00:03:06,930 --> 00:03:12,310
Okay. Okay, Daddy. We'll see you tomorrow night. Okay, bye-bye.

36
00:03:13,940 --> 00:03:15,270
"We"?

37
00:03:15,820 --> 00:03:19,940
Are, uh, having dinner with my dad tomorrow night. I hope that's okay.

38
00:03:20,150 --> 00:03:22,400
Oh, shoot. Tomorrow's not good.

39
00:03:22,570 --> 00:03:25,070
I'm supposed to, um, fall off the Empire State Building...

40
00:03:25,240 --> 00:03:27,620
...and land on a bicycle with no seat, so...

41
00:03:30,250 --> 00:03:32,410
Ross, my father doesn't hate you.

42
00:03:32,580 --> 00:03:35,630
Please. He refers to me as "Wet-Head."

43
00:03:36,590 --> 00:03:39,250
Honey, he calls everybody by a nickname.

44
00:03:39,420 --> 00:03:42,720
Okay, look, I know. Just one dinner. Please, one night. For me. Please?

45
00:03:42,880 --> 00:03:45,720
I just want him to love you like I do.

46
00:03:46,260 --> 00:03:48,850
All right. Well, not exactly like I do.

47
00:03:49,520 --> 00:03:51,680
But if you do come to dinner...

48
00:03:51,850 --> 00:03:55,230
...I'll love you like I do in that black thing that you like.

49
00:03:55,770 --> 00:03:57,360
I'll go.

50
00:04:00,990 --> 00:04:03,490
Fine.

51
00:04:00,990 --> 00:04:03,490
Thank you.

52
00:04:07,370 --> 00:04:08,490
Hi, Gunther.

53
00:04:08,660 --> 00:04:10,700
Yeah. We'll see.

54
00:04:13,160 --> 00:04:14,330
Hey, you guys.

55
00:04:13,160 --> 00:04:14,330
Hey.

56
00:04:14,500 --> 00:04:16,080
Hey. Hey.

57
00:04:14,500 --> 00:04:16,080
Guess what.

58
00:04:16,250 --> 00:04:17,880
I got a gig.

59
00:04:16,250 --> 00:04:17,880
Great.

60
00:04:18,460 --> 00:04:21,590
See, that's why I can never be an actor. Because I can't say "gig."

61
00:04:21,760 --> 00:04:25,010
Yeah, I can't say "croissant." Oh, my God.

62
00:04:26,430 --> 00:04:28,800
What's the part?

63
00:04:26,430 --> 00:04:28,800
It's not a part. No.

64
00:04:28,970 --> 00:04:31,970
I'm teaching Acting for Soap Operas at the Learning Extension.

65
00:04:32,140 --> 00:04:33,390
Come on. That's great.

66
00:04:33,560 --> 00:04:36,140
It's like my chance to give something back...

67
00:04:36,310 --> 00:04:38,480
...to the acting community.

68
00:04:38,770 --> 00:04:41,900
You know, you're probably not allowed to sleep with any of your students.

69
00:04:46,950 --> 00:04:48,530
I know.

70
00:04:54,210 --> 00:04:56,790
Ugh. I don't know, Monica. It feels funny just being here.

71
00:04:56,960 --> 00:04:59,750
I mean, if you buy a bed from Janice's ex-husband...

72
00:04:59,920 --> 00:05:01,670
...that's like betraying Chandler.

73
00:05:01,880 --> 00:05:04,050
Not at these prices.

74
00:05:09,140 --> 00:05:10,180
Hi.

75
00:05:10,390 --> 00:05:14,100
You know, in England this car would be on the other side of the store.

76
00:05:24,940 --> 00:05:26,820
Phoebe, come here.

77
00:05:26,990 --> 00:05:28,950
Ah, this is my new bed.

78
00:05:29,200 --> 00:05:31,910
You gotta feel this bad boy.

79
00:05:32,080 --> 00:05:35,200
Ugh. Monica, it still feels so weird, you know?

80
00:05:35,370 --> 00:05:36,870
Chandler's your friend. Oh.

81
00:05:38,250 --> 00:05:39,580
Oh, my God.

82
00:05:40,500 --> 00:05:45,380
Oh. All right, take this bed. You can make other friends. Oh.

83
00:05:53,600 --> 00:05:55,100
Good evening.

84
00:05:55,270 --> 00:05:57,770
I am Mr. Tribbiani.

85
00:05:59,150 --> 00:06:01,770
And I will be teaching Acting for Soap Operas.

86
00:06:02,230 --> 00:06:03,820
Now, um...

87
00:06:04,150 --> 00:06:09,400
...on my first day as Dr. Drake Ramoray on Days of our Lives...

88
00:06:12,280 --> 00:06:15,330
...uh, ahem, I learned that one of the most important things...

89
00:06:15,500 --> 00:06:18,500
...in soap opera acting is reacting.

90
00:06:18,830 --> 00:06:21,380
This does not mean acting again.

91
00:06:22,880 --> 00:06:26,050
It means you don't have a line, but someone else just did.

92
00:06:26,210 --> 00:06:28,590
And it goes something like this. Ahem.

93
00:06:35,640 --> 00:06:37,020
Thanks. Thanks. Thanks a lot.

94
00:06:37,230 --> 00:06:40,020
By the way, before I forget. To work in soap operas...

95
00:06:40,190 --> 00:06:43,230
...some of you are gonna have to become much more attractive.

96
00:06:44,520 --> 00:06:46,150
All right, moving right along...

97
00:06:50,360 --> 00:06:51,860
Hi.

98
00:06:50,360 --> 00:06:51,860
Hey.

99
00:06:52,030 --> 00:06:55,660
Oh! How was teaching last night?

100
00:06:52,030 --> 00:06:55,660
Oh, it was great. You get to say stuff like:

101
00:06:55,870 --> 00:07:00,410
"Hey, the bell doesn't dismiss you. I dismiss you."

102
00:07:01,670 --> 00:07:03,290
Oh, nice.

103
00:07:01,670 --> 00:07:03,290
Yeah.

104
00:07:03,460 --> 00:07:05,790
Oh, and guess what. I got an audition for All My Children.

105
00:07:06,090 --> 00:07:07,960
Yeah, it's this great part.

106
00:07:08,130 --> 00:07:09,670
This boxer named Nick.

107
00:07:09,840 --> 00:07:12,590
And I'm so, so right for it, you know? He's just like me...

108
00:07:12,760 --> 00:07:14,590
...except that he's a boxer...

109
00:07:14,760 --> 00:07:16,390
...and has an evil twin.

110
00:07:23,440 --> 00:07:26,690
Hear ye, hear ye. Delivery from the Mattress King.

111
00:07:29,360 --> 00:07:30,740
You Miss Geller?

112
00:07:30,900 --> 00:07:33,070
Okay.

113
00:07:30,900 --> 00:07:33,070
Sign here.

114
00:07:33,740 --> 00:07:36,030
Ooh. Do I have a middle name?

115
00:07:36,200 --> 00:07:39,370
All right. Monica Felula...

116
00:07:40,080 --> 00:07:41,290
...Geller.

117
00:07:41,460 --> 00:07:43,460
It's that bedroom there.

118
00:07:46,710 --> 00:07:49,210
Hey, Monica bought a bed from the Mattress King?

119
00:07:49,380 --> 00:07:52,720
Yeah. So please, please, please don't say anything to Chandler.

120
00:07:53,220 --> 00:07:55,680
You want me to lie to Chandler?

121
00:07:53,220 --> 00:07:55,680
Is that a problem?

122
00:07:55,850 --> 00:07:57,390
No.

123
00:07:58,390 --> 00:08:01,140
Oh, hey, hey, Nick the boxer. Let's see what you got.

124
00:08:01,310 --> 00:08:03,060
Yeah.

125
00:08:01,310 --> 00:08:03,060
All right, put them up. Come on.

126
00:08:03,230 --> 00:08:05,650
Hey, you're pretty good at this.

127
00:08:05,810 --> 00:08:07,940
Yeah, I had to learn. I was staying at the Y...

128
00:08:08,190 --> 00:08:10,730
...and some of the young men weren't acting Christian enough.

129
00:08:14,110 --> 00:08:16,200
Hey, now. Hey.

130
00:08:16,370 --> 00:08:19,330
Ow. And I'm bleeding. Okay, great.

131
00:08:19,540 --> 00:08:22,080
Wow. And I'm a vegetarian.

132
00:08:23,660 --> 00:08:25,710
All right, I'm sorry. We'll put some ice on it.

133
00:08:25,880 --> 00:08:27,960
Put your head back. Ooh.

134
00:08:25,880 --> 00:08:27,960
Okay. All right.

135
00:08:28,130 --> 00:08:29,750
I can't see.

136
00:08:28,130 --> 00:08:29,750
I have you.

137
00:08:29,920 --> 00:08:31,050
Oh, God.

138
00:08:32,590 --> 00:08:34,880
Which bedroom do you want it in, Ms. Geller?

139
00:08:35,050 --> 00:08:38,300
Oh. It's the compulsively neat one by the window, okay?

140
00:08:38,470 --> 00:08:40,100
Gotcha.

141
00:08:47,100 --> 00:08:48,690
Hi, Daddy.

142
00:08:49,150 --> 00:08:50,650
This is where they put us?

143
00:08:50,820 --> 00:08:53,780
What? There was no table available in the kitchen?

144
00:08:54,990 --> 00:08:56,610
Hello, baby.

145
00:08:54,990 --> 00:08:56,610
You remember Ross.

146
00:08:56,780 --> 00:08:58,780
Nice to see you again, Dr. Green.

147
00:08:59,780 --> 00:09:01,120
So.

148
00:09:06,670 --> 00:09:09,000
How's the library?

149
00:09:06,670 --> 00:09:09,000
Uh, museum.

150
00:09:09,920 --> 00:09:13,340
What happened to the library?

151
00:09:09,920 --> 00:09:13,340
There never was a library.

152
00:09:13,510 --> 00:09:16,130
I mean, there are libraries. It's just that I, um...

153
00:09:16,300 --> 00:09:18,800
I've never worked at one. Ha, ha.

154
00:09:21,850 --> 00:09:23,600
You know what's really good here?

155
00:09:23,770 --> 00:09:25,140
The lobster.

156
00:09:25,310 --> 00:09:29,560
What do you say? Shall I just order three?

157
00:09:25,310 --> 00:09:29,560
Yeah, if you're really hungry. Ha, ha.

158
00:09:34,110 --> 00:09:36,110
It was a joke. I made a joke.

159
00:09:37,450 --> 00:09:40,240
Yeah, actually, Daddy, Ross is allergic to lobster.

160
00:09:40,410 --> 00:09:42,080
What kind of person's allergic to lobster?

161
00:09:42,240 --> 00:09:45,000
I guess the kind of person that works in a library. Ha, ha.

162
00:09:45,160 --> 00:09:46,830
It's not...

163
00:09:45,160 --> 00:09:46,830
I know. It's a museum.

164
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:49,670
What, you're the only who can make a joke? At least mine was funny.

165
00:09:50,040 --> 00:09:51,840
Uh, waiter.

166
00:09:52,000 --> 00:09:54,960
We will have two lobsters and a menu.

167
00:09:59,180 --> 00:10:01,640
So, Dr. Green, how's the old boat?

168
00:10:01,800 --> 00:10:03,680
They found rust.

169
00:10:03,850 --> 00:10:07,810
Do you know what rust does to a boat?

170
00:10:03,850 --> 00:10:07,810
Gives it a nice antique-y look?

171
00:10:12,820 --> 00:10:16,440
Rust is boat cancer, Ross.

172
00:10:16,610 --> 00:10:18,070
Wow, I'm sorry.

173
00:10:18,320 --> 00:10:20,570
When I was a kid, I lost a bike to that. Ha, ha.

174
00:10:26,790 --> 00:10:28,370
Excuse me for a moment, will you, please?

175
00:10:28,540 --> 00:10:30,870
I wanna say good night to the Levines before we go.

176
00:10:31,170 --> 00:10:32,710
Okay.

177
00:10:35,710 --> 00:10:37,880
Honey, stop. It's not that bad.

178
00:10:38,220 --> 00:10:40,010
Yeah. Oh.

179
00:10:40,180 --> 00:10:42,720
Uh-oh. I think your dad must have added wrong.

180
00:10:42,890 --> 00:10:44,550
He only tipped, like, 4 percent.

181
00:10:44,720 --> 00:10:46,560
Yeah. Ahem, that's Daddy.

182
00:10:47,020 --> 00:10:51,310
"That's Daddy"? Doesn't it bother you? You're a waitress.

183
00:10:51,520 --> 00:10:53,100
Yes, it bothers me, Ross.

184
00:10:53,270 --> 00:10:55,570
But, you know, if he was a regular at the coffee house...

185
00:10:55,730 --> 00:10:57,400
...I'd be serving him sneezers.

186
00:10:58,490 --> 00:11:00,150
So?

187
00:10:58,490 --> 00:11:00,150
So, Ross...

188
00:11:00,320 --> 00:11:04,320
...I've bugged him about this a million times. He's not gonna change.

189
00:11:04,490 --> 00:11:06,870
Do you really serve people sneezers?

190
00:11:07,790 --> 00:11:09,660
Ahem, well, um, I don't.

191
00:11:10,870 --> 00:11:12,670
All right, kids. Ready?

192
00:11:10,870 --> 00:11:12,670
Yes.

193
00:11:12,830 --> 00:11:15,630
Thanks again, Dr. Green.

194
00:11:20,550 --> 00:11:23,180
Wait, wait, wait, I think I forgot my receipt.

195
00:11:23,340 --> 00:11:25,220
Oh, you don't need that.

196
00:11:23,340 --> 00:11:25,220
Ahem, why not?

197
00:11:25,390 --> 00:11:29,560
The carbon. It's messy. I mean, gets on your fingers and causes...

198
00:11:29,730 --> 00:11:31,270
...the, uh, night blindness.

199
00:11:34,610 --> 00:11:36,270
What is this?

200
00:11:36,610 --> 00:11:38,320
Who put a 20 down here?

201
00:11:38,480 --> 00:11:40,740
Oh, yeah, that would be me.

202
00:11:40,900 --> 00:11:42,950
Um, I have... I have a problem.

203
00:11:43,110 --> 00:11:45,280
I tip way too much, way, way too much.

204
00:11:45,450 --> 00:11:47,530
It's a sickness, really.

205
00:11:45,450 --> 00:11:47,530
Yeah, it is. It is.

206
00:11:47,700 --> 00:11:50,790
We have to do something about that.

207
00:11:47,700 --> 00:11:50,790
Excuse me.

208
00:11:51,120 --> 00:11:53,040
You think I'm cheap?

209
00:11:53,210 --> 00:11:55,500
He didn't mean anything by that.

210
00:11:55,670 --> 00:11:59,420
He really didn't.

211
00:11:55,670 --> 00:11:59,420
Nothing I do means anything. Really.

212
00:12:01,470 --> 00:12:03,930
This is nice. I pay $200 for dinner.

213
00:12:04,090 --> 00:12:07,430
You put down 20 and you come out looking like Mr. Big-Shot.

214
00:12:07,640 --> 00:12:09,350
You really wanna be Mr. Big-Shot?

215
00:12:09,520 --> 00:12:14,390
Here. I'll tell you what. You pay the whole bill, Mr. Big-Shot. All right?

216
00:12:16,900 --> 00:12:20,610
Well, "Mr. Big-Shot" is better than "Wet-Head."

217
00:12:21,900 --> 00:12:24,320
Okay, some tricks of the trade.

218
00:12:24,490 --> 00:12:26,820
Now, I've never been able to cry as an actor...

219
00:12:26,990 --> 00:12:29,580
...so if I'm in a scene where I have to cry...

220
00:12:29,740 --> 00:12:31,660
...I cut a hole in my pocket...

221
00:12:31,830 --> 00:12:34,040
...take a pair of tweezers...

222
00:12:34,420 --> 00:12:36,330
...and just start pulling.

223
00:12:38,750 --> 00:12:42,010
Okay. Or uh... Or let's say I want to convey...

224
00:12:42,170 --> 00:12:45,090
...that I've, uh, just done something evil.

225
00:12:45,260 --> 00:12:49,260
Well, that would be your basic I've-got-a fish-hook-in-my-eyebrow-and-I-like-it.

226
00:12:53,520 --> 00:12:57,020
No? Okay. Let's say I've just gotten bad news.

227
00:12:57,190 --> 00:13:02,360
Well, all I do there is try and divide 232 by 13.

228
00:13:11,330 --> 00:13:12,790
And that's how it's done.

229
00:13:12,950 --> 00:13:15,330
Great soap opera acting tonight, everybody.

230
00:13:15,500 --> 00:13:17,250
Class dismissed.

231
00:13:19,290 --> 00:13:20,960
Hey, Mr. Trib.

232
00:13:19,290 --> 00:13:20,960
Hey.

233
00:13:21,130 --> 00:13:23,170
Guess what. I got an audition.

234
00:13:23,340 --> 00:13:26,800
Oh. One of my students got an audition. I'm so proud.

235
00:13:28,340 --> 00:13:30,890
I was wondering if you would consider coaching me for it.

236
00:13:31,060 --> 00:13:33,890
Oh, you bet. What's the part?

237
00:13:31,060 --> 00:13:33,890
Oh, it's great.

238
00:13:34,060 --> 00:13:36,890
It's a role on All My Children. Nick the boxer.

239
00:13:45,740 --> 00:13:47,900
Had to do it, didn't you? You couldn't leave it alone.

240
00:13:48,070 --> 00:13:52,580
Four percent, okay? I tip more than that when there's a bug in my food.

241
00:13:52,740 --> 00:13:55,620
Ross, tonight was about the two of you getting along.

242
00:13:57,080 --> 00:13:58,620
Would you see my chiropractor already?

243
00:13:58,790 --> 00:14:02,880
Yeah, I'm gonna go to a doctor who went to medical school in a mini-mall.

244
00:14:07,220 --> 00:14:09,220
Hey, Pheebs. What are you doing?

245
00:14:09,390 --> 00:14:12,220
I'm... I'm freaking out.

246
00:14:14,270 --> 00:14:18,140
Monica kind of trusted me with something and she shouldn't have.

247
00:14:18,310 --> 00:14:21,270
All right, I haven't lived here in a while, so I have to ask you.

248
00:14:21,440 --> 00:14:23,820
Does Monica still turn the lights on in her bedroom?

249
00:14:25,610 --> 00:14:27,900
Ahem. Yeah.

250
00:14:25,610 --> 00:14:27,900
I am so dead.

251
00:14:30,530 --> 00:14:32,450
All right, look, here's the bottom line, Ross.

252
00:14:32,620 --> 00:14:35,160
This is fixable if we act fast, okay?

253
00:14:35,330 --> 00:14:38,120
So I'll invite him to brunch tomorrow and you can make nice.

254
00:14:38,290 --> 00:14:41,540
Honey, I have tried to make nice. It doesn't work.

255
00:14:41,750 --> 00:14:43,880
Okay. Look, I realize my father is difficult...

256
00:14:44,040 --> 00:14:47,670
...but that's why you have got to be the bigger man here.

257
00:14:47,840 --> 00:14:51,550
Look, sweetie, I could be the bigger man. I could be the biggest man.

258
00:14:51,760 --> 00:14:55,430
I could be a big, huge, giant man and it still wouldn't make a difference.

259
